AEM Forms: PDF Form, HTML Form & Adaptive Form

Today, I learned about the three types of forms used in Adobe Experience Manager (AEM).

1. PDF Form

- Used to collect information through a PDF document.
- Users can fill in fields such as name, email, and address.
- Useful for printable forms and document-based workflows.

2. HTML Form

- A form created using HTML elements such as "<form>", "<input>", and "<button>".
- Used to collect user input directly on a website.
- Example: Login form, registration form, and contact form.

3. Adaptive Form

- An AEM form designed to adapt its layout to different screen sizes.
- Supports form fields, validation, conditional visibility, and submission workflows.
- Example: Online application form, customer registration, and service request form.

🔍 Key Differences

Feature| PDF Form| HTML Form| Adaptive Form
Format| PDF document| Web page| AEM form experience
Responsive layout| Limited| Depends on design| Supported
Validation| Depends on PDF tools| HTML/JavaScript| Configurable form rules
Main use| Documents| Website input| Interactive data collection

💡 What I Learned

Each form serves a different purpose. The right choice depends on how users interact with the form and how the collected information needs to be processed.

